2025-09-10 01:38:38,062 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/responses', 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-f951e2f9-3ea5-43b5-8de5-97eb5b2c63df', 'json_data': {'input': [{'role': 'user', 'content': [{'type': 'input_text', 'text': '\nYou are an expert data scientist and simulation modeler. Your task is to analyze data for calibrating a simulation model.\n\nTASK DESCRIPTION:\nDevelop a multi-agent simulation system that models the spread of mask-wearing behavior through social networks.\n\nEVALUATION METRICS:\nNo metrics specified\n\nDATA SUMMARIES:\n- Data structure and type\n  - CSV of individual-level records; one row per agent.\n  - Mixed types: integers (agent_id, age, connection counts), categorical strings (age_group, occupation), float in [0,1] (risk_perception), boolean (initial_mask_wearing).\n\n- Meaning of columns\n  - agent_id: Unique agent identifier (row key).\n  - age, age_group: Demographics; age_group is derived from age.\n  - occupation: Socio-role; includes “Student,” “Blue Collar,” “White Collar,” etc.\n  - risk_perception: Normalized propensity (0–1) to value/adhere to protective behavior; higher values imply lower adoption threshold or higher compliance probability.\n  - initial_mask_wearing: Seed state for behavior at t0.\n  - family_connections, work_school_connections, community_connections: Intended degrees per social layer; counts of contacts in each network layer.\n  - total_connections: Expected overall degree; should equal the sum of the three layer-specific counts.\n\n- Relationships or nested elements\n  - No nested structures; flat schema.\n  - Derived fields:\n    - age_group from age.\n    - total_connections ≈ family + work_school + community (consistency check).\n  - Implicit multi-layer network: three contact layers (family, work/school, community) per agent; edges are not provided—only target degrees.\n\n- How this data should inform simulation entities or interactions\n  - Agent initialization:\n    - Create one agent per row with attributes: demographics, occupation, risk_perception, initial_mask_wearing.\n  - Network construction:\n    - Build three layer-specific graphs using the provided degree sequences (e.g., configuration model per layer), then combine into a multiplex network.\n    - Optionally constrain pairing by attributes (e.g., higher assortativity by household for family, by occupation/age_group for work/school).\n  - Interaction scheduling:\n    - Use layer-specific connection counts to set contact frequencies and weights; family > work/school > community or as calibrated.\n  - Behavior update rule:\n    - Social influence: probability of adopting or maintaining mask-wearing depends on the proportion of masked neighbors per layer, weighted by layer importance.\n    - Individual heterogeneity: risk_perception modulates susceptibility (e.g., higher risk_perception lowers required social threshold or increases adoption probability).\n    - Demographic/role effects: age_group and occupation can adjust layer weights (e.g., students emphasize school layer) or baseline propensity.\n    - Seed state: initial_mask_wearing sets starting adopters for diffusion dynamics.\n  - Validation/processing notes:\n    - Verify total_connections equals sum of layer counts; ensure counts are non-negative integers.\n    - Convert booleans from CSV correctly.\n    - Since explicit edges, households, and workplaces are absent, synthesize group assignments consistent with degrees and attributes.\n\nSemantic metadata summary for social_network.json\n\n- Overall data structure and type\n  - JSON object (dictionary) where each top-level key is an agent identifier (as a stringified integer).\n  - Each agent maps to an object containing multiple adjacency lists of other agent IDs (integers).\n\n- Meaning of keys or columns\n  - Top-level keys (e.g., "0", "1", "2"): unique agent IDs. Convert to integers for modeling convenience.\n  - Nested keys per agent:\n    - family: list of agent IDs with familial ties.\n    - work_school: list of agent IDs connected via workplace or school.\n    - community: list of agent IDs connected via broader community interactions.\n    - all: union of the above three lists (no self, duplicates removed).\n\n- Relationships or nested elements\n  - Multi-layer (multiplex) social network: three distinct relationship layers plus a precomputed union.\n  - Adjacency lists specify neighbors by layer; edges are unweighted and static in this file. Reciprocity is plausible but not guaranteed—verify if the graph is undirected (i.e., if A lists B, ensure B lists A) or enforce symmetry during ingestion.\n  - No timestamps, contact frequencies, or strengths are provided; any weighting must be imposed by the simulation.\n  - Potential data hygiene considerations: duplicates, self-loops, or missing agents should be checked; “all” can be recomputed from the three layers for validation.\n\n- How this data should inform simulation entities or interactions\n  - Agents: one agent per top-level key. Each agent holds:\n    - State variables (e.g., mask-wearing status, propensity/threshold, susceptibility).\n    - Layer-specific neighbor sets for family, work/school, and community.\n  - Interactions and influence:\n    - Use “family”, “work_school”, and “community” to model layer-specific influence with different weights or contact probabilities (e.g., family > work_school > community).\n    - Use “all” for rules that do not distinguish layers or for performance when aggregating influence quickly.\n    - Policies can target layers (e.g., workplace mandates) by modifying influence or compliance parameters on that layer only.\n    - Seeding strategies can leverage degree (neighbors count) within layers to target hubs.\n  - Network construction:\n    - Build a multiplex graph: three edge sets over the same node set.\n    - Optionally collapse to a single-layer graph using “all” when layer distinctions are unnecessary.\n    - Validate/repair symmetry if an undirected model is desired; otherwise, treat as directed influence edges.\n  - Dynamics:\n    - At each time step, compute adoption probability or threshold crossing from neighbors’ current states, aggregated per layer with chosen weights.\n    - The absence of edge weights or frequencies implies the model must define contact rates or influence strengths externally.\n\nSemantic metadata summary for train_data.csv\n\n- Data structure and type\n  - CSV file; each row is an observation for one agent on one discrete day (panel/time-series data).\n  - Columns: day (int), agent_id (int), wearing_mask (bool), received_info (bool).\n\n- Meaning of keys/columns\n  - day: Discrete timestep index for the simulation timeline.\n  - agent_id: Unique identifier for an agent (node) in the social network.\n  - wearing_mask: Agent’s behavioral state at that timestep; true if wearing a mask.\n  - received_info: Agent’s information state at that timestep; true if the agent received mask-related information during or before that day.\n\n- Relationships or nested elements\n  - No nesting; flat records.\n  - One-to-many relation: each agent_id has multiple rows across days (longitudinal trajectories).\n  - Temporal dependency: wearing_mask and received_info can change over time; past states may influence future states.\n  - No network topology provided; social connections must come from another source.\n\n- How this data should inform simulation entities or interactions\n  - Agent state variables:\n    - Behavior: wearing_mask(t)\n    - Information: received_info(t) and possibly memory of prior exposure\n  - Events/transitions to calibrate:\n    - Adoption: P(wearing_mask(t)=1 | wearing_mask(t-1)=0, received_info, neighbor behavior)\n    - Disadoption/decay: P(wearing_mask(t)=0 | wearing_mask(t-1)=1, …)\n    - Information receipt: P(received_info(t)=1 | network exposures/broadcast)\n  - Parameter estimation:\n    - Derive transition probabilities/lag structures from observed sequences (e.g., hazard of adopting after info, persistence of wearing).\n    - Estimate baseline adoption without info vs. with info to separate social/broadcast effects.\n  - Initialization:\n    - Use earliest day per agent for initial states.\n    - Map booleans to 0/1 for modeling.\n  - Validation:\n    - Compare simulated aggregate adoption curves and per-agent switch rates against this training data.\n  - Missing components to supply externally:\n    - Social network graph, info diffusion mechanism, and any exogenous interventions/broadcast schedules.\n\nCALIBRATION CONTEXT:\n{\n  "task_description": "Develop a multi-agent simulation system that models the spread of mask-wearing behavior through social networks.",\n  "metrics": [],\n  "file_purposes": {}\n}\n\nBased on the provided data summaries and task description, please analyze the data and provide insights for simulation model calibration.\nYour analysis should cover:\n\n1. What key patterns, distributions, and relationships exist in the data?\n2. How should this data be used to calibrate the simulation model?\n3. What parameters can be extracted from the data for configuring the simulation?\n4. What simulation design recommendations would you make based on this data?\n\nProvide your response in the following JSON format:\n```json\n{\n  "data_summary": {\n    "key_patterns": [\n      {"name": "Pattern Name", "description": "Description of the pattern", "relevance": "Why this matters for the simulation"}\n    ],\n    "key_distributions": [\n      {"name": "Distribution Name", "description": "Description of the distribution", "parameters": "Parameters that define this distribution"}\n    ],\n    "key_relationships": [\n      {"variables": ["var1", "var2"], "relationship": "Description of the relationship", "strength": "Description of strength"}\n    ]\n  },\n  "simulation_parameters": {\n    "parameter_category_1": {\n      "parameter_name_1": {\n        "value": "Extracted or recommended value",\n        "source": "Which data file and features this comes from",\n        "confidence": "High/Medium/Low",\n        "notes": "Any additional notes about this parameter"\n      }\n    }\n  },\n  "calibration_strategy": {\n    "preprocessing_steps": [\n      {"step": "Step description", "purpose": "Why this step is necessary"}\n    ],\n    "calibration_approach": "Description of overall approach to calibration",\n    "validation_strategy": "How to validate the calibrated model",\n    "key_variables_to_calibrate": ["var1", "var2", "var3"]\n  }\n}\n```\n\nProvide only valid JSON that can be parsed. Don\'t include any other explanation or text outside the JSON.\n'}]}], 'max_output_tokens': 10000, 'model': 'gpt-5-2025-08-07'}}
